217
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:11,040
Yes.

218
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:12,960
I felt like it was the rematch.

219
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:13,600
We were pumped.

220
00:13:13,680 --> 00:13:14,800
We were in a different mindset.

221
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:18,640
We tend to be very chill people, you know, very thoughtful and we hear people out.

222
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:20,480
You know, here we were ready more for a fight.

223
00:13:20,720 --> 00:13:20,800
Yeah.

224
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:21,840
It's interesting.

225
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,840
The interview is I don't know what their exact philosophy of it, but it's 50% constitution and intelligence test, right?

226
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:30,160
They want to just see how fast you are on your feet.

227
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:33,760
Maybe only half of it's probing the idea and your preparation.

228
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:40,240
If they can defeat your idea in thirty seconds or ten minutes, then like you're not ready, right?

229
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:41,520
You need to be ahead.

230
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,720
And you made some point there about building Streama, like kind of on your own and sort of your own bubble.

231
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:57,360
And when people are considering this like bootstrap not whatever, I do think that outside capital or outside pressure can be really helpful for a company.

232
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:08,400
I found it really helpful for me when I'm not sure what to do next or like not sure how fast to go or how slow to go whatever, like having a little bit of that constraint or interactions with those investor types or whatever, I found can be really clarifying.

233
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:10,080
It forces a little bit of discipline, right?

234
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:14,400
That forced you to go back and clarify your thinking and really be able to defend your idea.

235
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:16,080
Did you have the same interviewer both times?

236
00:14:16,240 --> 00:14:17,680
They probably don't remember anyway.

237
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:19,760
Different interviewer, but the second one was tough.

238
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:20,640
It was Michael Siebel.

239
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:22,720
I'm sure they're all tough.

240
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:25,120
I don't think I've never heard anyone say like I breezed through it.

241
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:29,040
So not to do too much YC inside baseball, but yeah, it's an intense experience.

242
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:33,280
I've never been in a more loaded atmosphere than like sitting outside the interview rooms.

243
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:39,600
Like I've never seen so many nervous, over achieving kids in my life outside of the YC interview.

244
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
And you get all sorts of dynamics and vibes.

245
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:45,760
The first time I remember, like some folks started interviewing me or like really me on my channel.

246
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:46,400
Well, that was me.

247
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:46,960
I was the guy.

248
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:47,440
I was the guy.

249
00:14:47,440 --> 00:14:47,520
I was the guy.

250
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:50,080
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

251
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:52,160
Parrying out in the hallway because I was so nervous.

252
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:53,280
It's so silly now.

253
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:59,040
But like I brought my mother-in-law or something, had given me some peppermint-sweeting oils that were like supposed to calm.

254
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:02,720
You just supposed to put a little bit on like your body and then you smell it and then it's supposed to go.

255
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:04,160
But no, I just brought the whole bottle with me.

256
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:05,840
And the whole time I was just like You're drinking it.

257
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:07,920
I was just like hugging it like a crazy person.

258
00:15:08,640 --> 00:15:15,840
One thing you talked about in the first interview was they pressing you on, well, Apple has a podcast app, Google has a podcast app, Spotify.

259
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,920
I don't know if they were big in podcasts at the time you applied, but this is a huge deal for apps.

260
00:15:20,080 --> 00:15:21,840
Like how big can your app really be?

261
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:27,360
There may be a huge ten, but it's also super competitive going into that second interview.

262
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:29,840
Did you prep for that question or how did that go?

263
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:31,680
And how do you think about it even to this day?

264
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,480
Like, how big can the podcast at be?

265
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:36,000
That's a really good question.

266
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:39,280
So there's this idea of bootstrap versus venture packed.

267
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:44,160
And I think this idea of deciding how to do it or what to go go goes beyond like the market size.

268
00:15:44,320 --> 00:15:49,280
I think it's also like a matter of what you're signing up for, you know, and what kind of entrepreneurial adventure you want to go through.

269
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:51,440
We're coming right after coming off this bootstrap.

270
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:52,480
So let's have that conversation, right?

271
00:15:52,720 --> 00:15:53,360
So like, okay.

272
00:15:53,680 --> 00:15:54,320
Did you guys know?

273
00:15:54,880 --> 00:15:56,560
Because I think that was the thing I didn't know.

274
00:15:57,040 --> 00:16:02,160
Honestly, even when we did YC, clarify to me during YC what you're signing up for for.

275
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:05,520
You go from building cash flow business first to building equities business first.

276
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:10,320
You're engaging in business with some people who, you know, essentially invest in equities for living, right?

277
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:14,640
Just like any other customer, they're going to like spend some money and expect some kind of return.

278
00:16:15,120 --> 00:16:19,760
Did you know that or I mean, I'll say for me, I think I was stuck in the lights a little bit, right?

279
00:16:19,920 --> 00:16:21,040
As like, this is what you do.

280
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:23,040
This is what all these founders I look up to do.

281
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:26,080
Like, where were you at in that mental game?

282
00:16:26,720 --> 00:16:28,080
I think it was mixed feelings.

283
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:34,960
I definitely was thinking a lot about it because we were very jaded or skewed, I guess, by a ten year bootstrapping experience.

284
00:16:35,200 --> 00:16:37,760
And so we were constantly thinking, should we have done it differently?

285
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:38,640
Is this a better path?

286
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:40,480
So I definitely was thinking a lot about it.

287
00:16:40,560 --> 00:16:43,040
And to this day, I still think a lot about it because we've raised some money.

288
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:44,320
We raised like a million and a half.

289
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:45,600
So we didn't raise a lot of money.

290
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:48,080
And that option is still on the table for us.

291
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:50,480
It's not that we said, hey, we're never going to raise money again.

292
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:53,840
But I do wonder, will that be the best path?

293
00:16:54,160 --> 00:16:56,080
So at the time, yes, we were thinking about it.

294
00:16:56,560 --> 00:17:03,360
I think one of the main worries for us was, is this something that's going to compromise our desire to build something for the future that can last?

295
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:06,320
Is it going to limit our options too much?

296
00:17:06,960 --> 00:17:08,400
Is it going to compromise our lifestyle?

297
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:09,840
You hear all these horror stories.

298
00:17:09,920 --> 00:17:12,880
I love working hard and I do think that work is my hobby to some extent.

299
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:14,240
Sounds kind of sad.

300
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:14,880
No, no, no.

301
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:15,600
It makes us happy.

302
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:19,440
But I do feel like there's a kind of craftsmanship around being an entrepreneur and building things.

303
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:20,960
But at the same time, I want to have my family.

304
00:17:21,200 --> 00:17:22,960
I want to do certain things that are important to me.

305
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:24,640
And so I guess I was afraid.

306
00:17:25,040 --> 00:17:28,800
And after hearing all these stories of folks getting burned out, things that sounded terrible.

307
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:33,040
But here's what I would say for sure, the more you bring outside folks, right?

308
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:35,760
Involving other people in the business, I think there's higher stakes.

309
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:36,960
There's more responsibility.

310
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:38,000
There's more accountability.

311
00:17:38,080 --> 00:17:39,920
I think to what you were saying earlier, Jacob, right?

312
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:42,240
You need to know if you're in the mood for that, right?

313
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:49,920
And if that's what you want to sign up for, or if you want to do something that's more your terms, your freedom, optionality, always.

314
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:54,720
Again, I don't want to get too like into the psychology, but I do think there's the issue of commitment.

315
00:17:54,880 --> 00:17:58,320
How comfortable do we all feel in commitment versus freedom?

316
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:00,160
I think there's a lot of parallels there.

317
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:02,320
I've never really had a bootstrapped business.

318
00:18:02,560 --> 00:18:04,240
I think everybody's grass is greener, right?

319
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:07,760
They're always thinking that the other one is, oh, that'll solve all my problems.

320
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,080
And the reality, it's somewhere messy middle.

321
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:16,720
I think the easiest thought process I'll give to most people is that, you know, with raising venture, it unequivocally accelerates your business, right?

322
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:18,080
You can make the hire today.

323
00:18:18,240 --> 00:18:19,280
You would have had to wait a year, right?

324
00:18:19,520 --> 00:18:22,160
You can build the thing today that you would have had to wait.

325
00:18:22,480 --> 00:18:27,840
And that can make the whole thing go a lot faster assuming you can find product market fit and you're actually building into a market that cares.

326
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:30,720
The venture capital can be really., really awesome.

327
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:33,360
And you don't have to build a wholly unhealthy business too, right?

328
00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:38,640
Like you can just be, you know, like RevenueCat, we operate maybe a year ahead of what we can afford, right?

329
00:18:38,880 --> 00:18:41,440
At any given time or so, plus or minus, depending on the quarter.

330
00:18:41,840 --> 00:18:45,120
Yeah, we burn venture, but also like the business is sound in many ways.

331
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:50,560
I think the thing that I always think about is that once you bring that money in, you're limiting your options.

332
00:18:51,200 --> 00:18:56,400
The number of all mutually agreed upon successes for the company is limited, right?

333
00:18:56,960 --> 00:18:58,000
Doesn't mean you're screwed.

334
00:18:58,240 --> 00:19:00,160
Doesn't mean you can't do certain things, right?

335
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:05,280
Like there are stories of venture backed companies that have returned to like a free cash flow and bootstraping model.

336
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:11,200
There are stories of companies that have, I don't know, I think Tyler's been pretty public about this, but like Gumroad is a good example.

337
00:19:11,440 --> 00:19:17,120
Like he raised a bunch from, I think it was Sequoia, and never really got the right product market fit.

338
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:21,440
And he just kind of went to them and was like, hey, I'm just not going to keep building this like you guys want.

339
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:23,040
And they kind of just cashed out.

340
00:19:23,200 --> 00:19:24,400
They were like, yeah, okay, that's fine.

341
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:24,720
Right.

342
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:27,200
They wrote it off and then he went and built it his own way.

343
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:27,520
Right.

344
00:19:27,680 --> 00:19:30,080
So there are options, but those can be rare.

345
00:19:30,560 --> 00:19:33,520
My advice would be, it's a bit reductive, but I think it's true.

346
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:39,120
It's like when you take money from people, just figure out how you're going to pay them back 510, 20x.

347
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:40,480
Like that's what they're going to want.

348
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:43,120
That's their success criteria is a 510, 20x payday.

349
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:49,200
And if you're not comfortable thinking about that math, then probably don't take that money.

350
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:49,920
Right?

351
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:51,600
And I saw this and it was helpful for me.

352
00:19:51,680 --> 00:19:53,840
It's like I was very timid about it initially.

353
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:54,960
I was like, oh, I don't know.

354
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:56,240
Like it's a little scary.

355
00:19:56,400 --> 00:20:00,480
And I think seed and early stage investors are very good at kind of being like, nah, you know, pushing a little bit.

356
00:20:00,800 --> 00:20:02,080
Like, yeah, yeah, you just take the money.

357
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:03,200
It's not a big deal.

358
00:20:03,280 --> 00:20:04,080
It's not a big deal.

359
00:20:04,160 --> 00:20:07,920
But as you get further along, like it gets a little bit there, like, I do want a return at some point.

360
00:20:08,160 --> 00:20:08,320
Right?

361
00:20:08,560 --> 00:20:10,400
Like, especially if you start to look like a winner.

362
00:20:10,560 --> 00:20:11,840
And that goes back to that accountability.

363
00:20:12,160 --> 00:20:12,640
It can be good.

364
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:14,080
I think for me, it's been good.

365
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:16,400
It's not just the advice I get from those folks.

366
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:20,320
It's the betting and then saying folks I know have other winners and being like, I think you have.

367
00:20:20,640 --> 00:20:22,400
the making of a winner in our portfolio.

368
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:23,920
So like, I want you to keep pushing.

369
00:20:24,240 --> 00:20:25,200
I find that helpful.

370
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:28,880
But I also for revenue cat, I couldn't be like 180.

371
00:20:28,960 --> 00:20:31,120
Actually, we're going to be a bootstrap total now.

372
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:33,840
Like, that's not viable anymore, which is fine.

373
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:36,000
It's okay in life to close doors sometimes.

374
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:43,120
I just, I think the advice that I would hope anyone in this situation, that's the thing you need to think about is like, okay, what doors am I closing?

375
00:20:43,280 --> 00:20:44,560
And am I okay closing those doors?

376
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:46,080
I don't think there's like a right answer.

377
00:20:46,160 --> 00:20:50,800
I mean, we've covered this on the podcasts before, but it's rare to talk to someone who's played both sides.

378
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:52,960
I think that's a great summary, man.

379
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:59,520
I also wonder again, this might be an example of grassy screening on the other side, but I do wonder if there's like a B2C versus B2B difference also.

380
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:00,320
Oh, absolutely.

381
00:21:00,480 --> 00:21:00,560
Yeah.

382
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:09,600
Like, I do get the feeling, of course, B2B companies, they pivot, they struggle with prior market fit, all these classic surprises I think that all companies have.

383
00:21:09,760 --> 00:21:11,600
But I do feel like B2C, I don't know.

384
00:21:11,760 --> 00:21:12,640
That's all my experience, right?

385
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:13,440
That's all I've done.

386
00:21:13,600 --> 00:21:18,720
And it's like, I don't know, you're trying to kind of read the culture a little bit, you know, what's going to happen in the world broadly.

387
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:24,160
I mean, we all do that as entrepreneurs, but I do think that there's something here that's less predictable, more artistic maybe.

388
00:21:24,320 --> 00:21:26,400
Like, yeah, oh, it's a hits business, right?

389
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:30,320
If you look at other hits businesses like the movies, they run a portfolio.

390
00:21:30,640 --> 00:21:34,000
Like, have a lot and a lot of movies and they lose a lot of money on a lot of movies and then they.

391
00:21:34,640 --> 00:21:35,600
have a couple that make a ton.

392
00:21:36,400 --> 00:21:43,520
And I think what that does is it creates this really dangerous and difficult early phase where you haven't had your first hit yet.

393
00:21:44,320 --> 00:21:51,200
And with B2B, if you can make one customer happy, like really happy, you have a foothold.

394
00:21:51,600 --> 00:21:54,640
And then you can make that too, and you can make that for, and like that's how we met.

395
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:59,360
Like I met you during YC when I was looking for those first couple of customers to make happy.

396
00:21:59,840 --> 00:22:01,280
I emailed you because you were in YC.

397
00:22:01,440 --> 00:22:05,360
I was like, can I just come put my stupid SDK in your app, please?

398
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:07,280
And you were gracious enough to be like, sure.

399
00:22:07,520 --> 00:22:09,600
And we hung out at a blue bottle for a few hours.

400
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:52,000
You sent some pull requests for us like including the sdk but i think it was a couple of bugs you know as long as you your car fix they ever put some like candy on the dash or a little bit of like appreciation gift that was my little appreciation gift i fixed some ui things i saw while i was in there and it was really cool man by the way because subscription wasn't big for us at the time right now it's most of our revenue and the company three x or four x because we properly took the time to like focus on subscription and at the time it was mostly ads and subscription was something that we had but just for the handful of people that wanted to remove the ads and so we gave them a subscription that was pretty hidden and it was already working and i think you added the sdk so we would do it through you but it ended up like planting the seeds for what would be truly the future of our business model and our revenue.

401
00:22:52,080 --> 00:22:54,240
So I won't take any credit for that.

402
00:22:54,480 --> 00:23:00,320
I will take credit for fixing the little launch UI bug, though, like the image being mis sized, that definitely I take credit for.

403
00:23:00,800 --> 00:23:08,560
But that era, you know, going back to the B2B, B2C, as a B2B founder, you can show up in a coffee shop and implement and learn and iterate.

404
00:23:08,640 --> 00:23:10,560
And like there's just much easier toe holds.

405
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:15,040
And then like the flywheels are present much sooner in a B2B business.

406
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:20,000
Also because of retention, our retention tends to be over 100%.

407
00:23:20,480 --> 00:23:21,520
It's just a easier game.

408
00:23:21,680 --> 00:23:25,040
And so going into that question, I would say it's not necessarily a easier game, but in some ways.

409
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:26,400
more predictable game.

410
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:30,640
And so going back to the raising question, I've definitely changed my tack here a lot.

411
00:23:30,880 --> 00:23:34,880
And I think for Consumer Founders, I think it's even a bigger game with B to B.

412
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:44,080
It's been pretty well shown how venture capital can be turned into like enterprise equity value and then eventually like a public company for B to C, that certainly exists.

413
00:23:44,240 --> 00:23:46,080
I mean, there's Duolingos of the world.

414
00:23:46,320 --> 00:23:49,040
There's lots of B to C and they're massively valuable.

415
00:23:49,120 --> 00:23:58,320
I mean, compared to even like your average B to B company in a lot of ways, but there's an order of magnitude or several orders of magnitude fewer of them than there are.

416
00:23:59,040 --> 00:24:00,160
And even Duolingo, right?

417
00:24:00,320 --> 00:24:05,040
I feel like it's so much more clearly at like an educational tool to learn a very concrete skill, which is amazing.

418
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:06,560
And I think it's amazing company, right?

419
00:24:06,720 --> 00:24:09,120
But I'm just comparing that to maybe like a clubhouse.

420
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:11,200
They raised, I think, I don't know, was it a billion?

421
00:24:11,280 --> 00:24:14,240
Like, they raised more money.

422
00:24:14,400 --> 00:24:16,880
Like, they killed the company, essentially, right?

423
00:24:17,120 --> 00:24:18,400
It put them in a really weird place.

424
00:24:18,880 --> 00:24:21,600
At the time they were really booming, the metrics were insane.

425
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:24,160
We were following it because we are in the audience space, right?

426
00:24:24,480 --> 00:24:25,360
And we were like, whoa.

427
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:27,440
And it looked like it could be the next big thing, right?

428
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:32,240
And then maybe, I don't know, lockdowns ease, COVID change, different things.

429
00:24:32,400 --> 00:24:36,160
And I don't know the exact metrics, but from public analytics, you can see that they have come way down.

430
00:24:36,480 --> 00:24:39,120
And as you're saying, like raising so much money limits your optionality.

431
00:24:39,520 --> 00:24:40,240
Where do you go from there?

432
00:24:40,400 --> 00:24:44,320
When you've raised so much money, you need to do like a 10x, 15x, 20x, how do you do that?

